.TH "DEDALUS\-LOGIN" "1" "" "dedalus" "Dedalus Manual"
.SH "NAME"
dedalus\-login \- sign in and save credentials
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.B dedalus login
[\fB\-\-flow\fR \fIname\fR] [\fB\-\-base\-url\fR \fIurl\fR]
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
Obtains a credential and saves it so later commands do not have to carry one. It goes into your operating system's credential store \- the system keyring on Linux, Credential Manager on Windows \- and falls back to a file in your state directory, readable only by you, when no such store is available. On macOS it is always that file, because the system's own tool accepts a password only on its command line, where other processes could read it. Either way it is filed under the base URL it was captured for, so a credential saved for one host is never sent to another. A credential passed with a flag, or set in the environment, still takes precedence over a saved one.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-\-flow\fR \fIname\fR
Sign\-in flow to use, skipping the prompt. One of: api\-key, x\-api\-key, bearer\-auth.
.TP
\fB\-\-base\-url\fR \fIurl\fR
Sign in against this base URL instead of the default.
.SH "SIGN\-IN FLOWS"
.TP
\fBapi\-key\fR
Enter your access token.
.TP
\fBx\-api\-key\fR
Enter your API key.
.TP
\fBbearer\-auth\fR
Enter your access token.
.SH "EXAMPLES"
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
dedalus login
dedalus login \-\-flow api\-key
.fi
.RE
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
\fB0\fR
The command completed successfully.
.TP
\fB2\fR
The command was invoked incorrectly (missing or invalid arguments).
.TP
\fB10\fR
Sign\-in or sign\-out did not complete. The message on standard error says why.
